     6	
     7	# Mistake
     8	
     9	Skill for every agent in every role. Loaded as the first domain skill in the Load Directives block of every delegation prompt. Its discipline spans two directions: **check before acting** (so known pitfalls are avoided) and **write immediately after correction** (so the correction survives across sessions).
    10	
    11	The model is **staging → promotion**. During the working loops, agents write mistake-candidates to session staging only. Promotion to project memory (`mistakes/` directories) is performed by agents during the Wrap-up phase (no CLI command). Working-loop agents never write directly to project memory; the Wrap-up assistant performing promotion during Wrap-up is the documented sole exception.
    12	
    13	---
    14	
    15	## Memory Access Matrix
    16	
    17	The agent MUST observe these tier boundaries. For working-loop agents, the only write surface is session staging. The Wrap-up assistant is the sole exception: it writes promoted candidates to project or feature `mistakes/` during the Wrap-up phase.
    18	
    19	| Memory tier | Path root | Access |
    20	|---|---|---|
    21	| **Project mistakes — project-level** | `.gobbi/projects/{project-name}/mistakes/` | **READ-ONLY** — load at the start of any work; never written by working-loop agents (Wrap-up assistant is the sole exception) |
    22	| **Feature mistakes** | `.gobbi/projects/{project-name}/features/{feature-name}/mistakes/` | **READ-ONLY** — load when the task is feature-scoped; never written by working-loop agents (Wrap-up assistant is the sole exception) |
    23	| **Session staging** | `sessions/{date}-{session-id}/{loop}/staging/decisions/{slug}.md` with frontmatter `mistake-candidate: true` | **WRITE (PASS only, during MEMORIZATION)** — the only surface agents write to; Wrap-up promotes to project or feature `mistakes/` based on scope confirmed with user |
    24	
    25	**Delete semantics**: agents NEVER delete mistake files in any tier. When a mistake is superseded, the new file carries `supersedes: <old-path>` frontmatter; the old file is updated in place with `status: superseded` + `superseded_by: <new-path>`.
    26	
    27	**Promotion**: The Wrap-up assistant promotes staged mistake-candidates to project memory during the Wrap-up phase (no CLI command). Promotion is NOT a context reload — agents do not re-read project mistakes after promotion; they read them at the start of the next session.
    28	
    29	---
    30	
    31	## Core Principles
    32	
    33	> **Check before acting.**
    34	
    35	Every agent reads the applicable mistakes before starting any non-trivial work. Mistakes are the highest-density knowledge in the system — a mistake read is a mistake avoided.
    36	
    37	> **Write immediately after correction.**
    38	
    39	When the user or an evaluator corrects an approach, the agent stages a mistake-candidate before the session ends. A correction not staged is a correction that will repeat across sessions.
    40	
    41	> **Include WHY and HOW-TO-APPLY.**
    42	
    43	A mistake without its cause and recovery pattern is unactionable. Every mistake entry must state: (1) what went wrong; (2) why it went wrong (the mistaken assumption); (3) how to recognize the situation before making the same mistake; (4) the corrected approach.
    44	
    45	> **Promotion happens during the Wrap-up phase — working-loop agents do not promote.**
    46	
    47	The Wrap-up assistant promotes staged mistake-candidates from session staging to project memory (`mistakes/`) during the Wrap-up phase. Working-loop agents write to session staging only and never write directly to `mistakes/`. The Wrap-up phase is the sole documented exception to the staging boundary.
    48	
    49	> **Supersede, never delete.**
    50	
    51	When a new mistake supersedes an older one (new understanding overrides the prior correction), write the new mistake with a `supersedes:` frontmatter pointer, and update the older file's frontmatter in place (`status: superseded`, `superseded_by: <new-path>`). Physical deletion is forbidden.
    52	
    53	---
    54	
    55	## Procedures
    56	
    57	### P1 — Load mistakes before starting work
    58	
    59	At the start of any task:
    60	
    61	1. Read `.gobbi/projects/{project-name}/mistakes/*.md` — all project-level mistake files.
    62	2. If the task is feature-scoped: read `.gobbi/projects/{project-name}/features/{feature-name}/mistakes/*.md`.
    63	3. Filter by domain relevance — load mistakes whose domain tag matches the task's domain (e.g., `docs-sync`, `process`, `security`, `hooks`).
    64	4. Note any applicable mistakes explicitly in the Study phase before making any decision in that domain.
    65	
    66	**Do not skim.** A mistake that is not read is a mistake that repeats.
    67	
    68	### P2 — Detect a correction during work
    69	
    70	During work (any phase), a correction is any of:
    71	
    72	- The user explicitly saying an approach is wrong and providing the right one.
    73	- An evaluator finding of type `design_flaw` or `assumption_risk` with confidence ≥ 75 that reveals a repeating pattern.
    74	- A wrong assumption the agent itself caught before the user noticed — record it; the discipline is the same.
    75	
    76	When a correction occurs:
    77	
    78	1. Stop and acknowledge it — do not silently adjust and continue.
    79	2. Note it as a mistake-candidate with: what went wrong, why (the mistaken assumption), how to recognize it next time, the corrected approach.
    80	3. Write the candidate note **immediately** — do not defer to MEMORIZATION. If the session is interrupted before MEMORIZATION runs, deferred notes are lost. This is the moment-of-capture discipline; see [`memorization/SKILL.md` § Core Principles § Moment-of-capture](../memorization/SKILL.md#core-principles) for the rationale and empirical witness.
    81	
    82	### P3 — Stage a mistake-candidate during MEMORIZATION
    83	
    84	During MEMORIZATION on PASS:
    85	
    86	1. For each correction noted during P2, write a staging file at `sessions/{date}-{session-id}/{loop}/staging/decisions/{slug}.md`.
    87	2. Stamp the file with the `decisions.md` template from `memorization/templates/decisions.md`.
    88	3. Set frontmatter `mistake-candidate: true`.
    89	4. Body must contain all four elements: what went wrong / why / how to recognize / corrected approach.
    90	5. Add a `domain:` frontmatter tag matching the mistake's domain (e.g., `docs-sync`, `process`, `security`, `hooks`).
    91	
    92	The slug is kebab-case derived from the primary symptom — not from "mistake" or the domain tag.
    93	
    94	### P4 — Wrap-up-phase promotion
    95	
    96	During the Wrap-up phase, the Wrap-up assistant promotes staged mistake-candidates from session staging into `mistakes/`. The next session's P1 load will pick them up. Working-loop agents never perform promotion themselves — staging is their sole write surface during the working loops.
    97	
    98	---
    99	
   100	## Constraints
   101	
   102	- **MUST load before any non-trivial work** — every delegation prompt's Load Directives block includes the `mistake` skill at position 3 (after `principles` and project rules).
   103	- **MUST read applicable mistakes at Study phase** — do not skip, do not filter to "what seems relevant today."
   104	- **MUST stage immediately after correction** — do not defer to the end of the loop; do not assume the session will complete normally.
   105	- **MUST NOT write directly to `mistakes/`** — working-loop agents never write to project memory; only the Wrap-up assistant writes to it (by promoting staged candidates during Wrap-up).
   106	- **MUST NOT delete** — supersede via frontmatter; physical deletion of any mistake file is forbidden.
   107	- **MUST include all four elements in every mistake entry** — what / why / how-to-recognize / corrected approach. Missing elements make the mistake unactionable.
   108	
   109	---
   110	
   111	## Output paths
   112	
   113	Staging-phase writes during MEMORIZATION follow the routing defined in `evaluation/SKILL.md` § Finding Metadata → Domain `process`.
   114	
   115	| Path | Written by | Written |
   116	|---|---|---|
   117	| `sessions/{date}-{session-id}/{loop}/staging/decisions/{slug}.md` (with `mistake-candidate: true`) | assistant (MEMORIZATION) | PASS only — one file per mistake-candidate, stamped with `decisions.md` template |
   118	
   119	Wrap-up reads these staging files and promotes to the destination based on user-confirmed scope:
   120	
   121	| Scope (user-confirmed) | Destination |
   122	|---|---|
   123	| Feature-scoped mistake | `.gobbi/projects/{project-name}/features/{feature-name}/mistakes/{slug}.md` |
   124	| Project-scoped mistake | `.gobbi/projects/{project-name}/mistakes/{slug}.md` |
   125	
   126	**Path conventions**
   127	
   128	- `{date}` — session start date in `YYYY-MM-DD`
   129	- `{session-id}` — Claude Code session ID supplied by the delegation prompt's `session-id:` header field (the parent session's id). Do NOT read `$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` for this value: in a spawned-subagent context that env-var holds the subagent's own UUID, not the parent session's.
   130	- `{loop}` — the loop during which the mistake was staged (`ideation` / `preparation` / `planning` / `execution` / `wrap-up`)
   131	- `{slug}` — kebab-case derived from the mistake's primary symptom (≤ 60 characters)
   132	- `{project-name}` — project slug from `session.json.project`
   133	- `{feature-name}` — feature slug from `session.json.feature`

